  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Data:** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` holds web-server access logs. It has:\n- `timestamp`\n- `bytes`\n- `response.keyword`, the HTTP status code\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os.keyword`\n- `extension.keyword`\n- `url.keyword`\n- `referer`\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-ef7e798e Web logs overview\" (id `53ec2eed-9a01-4ea3-aa37-0ffc79f08648`) has five panels:\n- Total request count\n- Average bytes\n- Total bytes\n- A line chart of requests over time\n- A bar chart of requests by response code\n\nI didn't open the dashboard in Kibana to check how the panels render. The create call returned the saved dashboard.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-ef7e798e 5xx spike\" (id `fed9e8e5-b3d7-4adc-b65f-035d1566dc3d`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Schedule:** it runs every 1 minute.\n- **Condition:** it fires when more than 10 documents match a 5xx response code (`response.keyword` matching `5[0-9][0-9]`) within a 5-minute window.\n- **Actions:** it has none, so a firing only shows up in Kibana. It sends no notification.",
